Title Benefits of routine CTG monitoring on admission of women in the maternity unit
Category Obstetrics / Gynecology > Obstetrics > Intrauterine fetal surveillance
(CD Flag as important
A 28-year-old woman is admitted to the Maternity Unit in early labor. She has had an
uncomplicated pregnancy to date and is otherwise healthy. She asks you about fetal monitoring
during labor and says she has heard that cardiotocography (CTG) is helpful for the assessment of
the baby''s well-being and preventing fetal problems. In counseling regarding the use of CTG as a
predictor of fetal outcome and satisfactory labor in comparison to intermittent auscultation and
whether CTG monitoring will reduce the risk of neonatal developmental abnormalities, which one of
